    "explanation": "Both parties: this case turns on one fact the record cannot settle, so the 5.00 SGD fee is held for human review rather than confirmed to the driver or returned to the rider.\n\nWhat is established. The driver reached the pickup at 11:11:50 — the trip record's auto-arrival is corroborated by the GPS trace, whose nearest fix sits 2.05 m from the pickup with 5 m reported accuracy, well inside the 50 m STANDARD radius and the 25 m accuracy filter (EV-GPS-05065, COMPLETE, weight 1.0). The trip was on demand with no scheduled time, so under POL-NS-21 the 300-second free wait ran from arrival and expired at 11:16:50; the driver cancelled at 11:18:10, 380 seconds after arrival and 80 seconds past the end of the wait. The driver also placed one in-app call at 11:14:20, unanswered after 20 seconds, before cancelling — the call half of POL-NS-23's contact obligation is met.\n\nWhat cannot be established. POL-NS-23 also requires at least one in-app chat message from the driver before cancelling, and it voids the fee if either half is missing. The only artifact that could show that message is the chat log, and it is impaired — issue code TRUNCATED_LOG, weight 0.6 (EV-CHAT-05065): 1 of the 3 messages the trip record reports was exported, and nothing after 11:06:00 survives, so the arrival and the entire waiting window are unrecorded. The log therefore cannot carry a finding that no message was sent, and equally cannot carry one that a message was sent. The rider's statement that she received no message does not settle it: POL-NS-23 tests whether the driver sent a message, not whether it was received, and a counterparty statement is not the artifact that clause names. The message limb is therefore undecided, and an undecided limb supports neither the fee (which needs the obligation met) nor a refund (which needs it failed). POL-GEN-26 governs exactly this situation — whether the driver messaged and called before cancelling is a fact POL-NS-23 needs and cannot be established from impaired evidence — and directs escalation with the open question stated.\n\nConsequences for each side's ask. The driver's request for DRIVER_COMPENSATION of 5.00 SGD cannot be granted, because POL-NS-22 pays only where the POL-NS-23 contact obligation is met and the message half is undecided. The rider's request that the fee be voided under POL-NS-23 likewise cannot be granted, because the impaired log cannot establish that a message was missing. The rider's fallback — that a driver-filed case should be resolved as NO_ACTION so the already-refunded state stands — is not available either: POL-GEN-03 makes NO_ACTION the finding that the filing party's claim is not supported, which would require deciding the message limb against the driver, and it is undecided.\n\nThe remaining clauses add nothing. POL-NS-24 does not apply (the rider never cancelled, and no scheduled time exists to fix its deadline, so no 5.00 SGD goodwill credit arises). POL-NS-22's fee-amount check is satisfied (EV-FARE-05065 charged exactly the 5.00 SGD policy fee, so no referral). POL-NS-25 does not fire (the rider profile carries no no_show_cancellations_30d counter, and the rule applies only when that field is present). No safety indicator appears in the record, and neither profile carries a fraud flag.\n\nReview and confidence. The dispute value is 5.00 SGD, below POL-GEN-22's 10.00 SGD band, and neither profile carries a fraud flag, so POL-GEN-22 and POL-NS-26 set no confidence bar to a no-show decision; the escalation rests on POL-GEN-26's missing fact, not on confidence. Because the ruling rests on the impaired chat log (weight 0.6), confidence is capped at 0.6. The 5.00 SGD is held: it is neither confirmed to the driver nor returned to the rider. The open question for the reviewer is whether the driver sent an in-app chat message to the rider before cancelling; the full chat export (3 entries) should be obtained."
  },
